  "verbalization": "Trans-urocanic acid appears to exert its biological effects through a series of mechanistic steps that ultimately target the CD276 antigen. The evidence suggests that trans-urocanic acid interacts with Microtubule-associated protein 1B, which is then targeted by a compound with the structure 6-(2-hydroxypropanoyl)-4a,7-dihydro-1H-pteridine-2,4-dione. This compound, in turn, has a direct target of the CD276 antigen. The presence of multiple pathways converging on the CD276 antigen indicates a potential role for trans-urocanic acid in modulating the expression or activity of this antigen. The final score of 0.486 in path_9226 and 0.4779 in path_9227 suggests a consistent and plausible relationship, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a possible role for trans-urocanic acid in influencing the CD276 antigen, but further experimental validation is needed to confirm the direct interaction and the functional significance of this relationship.",
